                <pb facs="0474" n="340" sameAs="frus:frus1888p1/pg340"/>

                <head>No. 224.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Denby</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-07-25"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 25,
                        1888</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 338.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have to acknowledge the receipt of your
                    dispatch, No. 646, of May 26 last, in regard to the exclusion of Chinese from
                    Australia.</p>
                <p>Your dispatch is interesting in its bearing on the general problem of Chinese
                    emigration and the competition of Chinese with domestic labor, which appears to
                    demand an effective solution in other countries besides the United States.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
